---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../lib/samples';
---
<Layout title="Authoring guide" description="Explore the library, adapt a sample, and write your own code explanation.">
  <div class="intro"><p class="eyebrow">Getting started</p><h1>Build an explanation,<br />one step at a time.</h1><p class="lede">A lesson combines the code students see with the teaching moments you want them to follow.</p></div>
  <div class="prose guide">
    <h2>Explore, then adapt</h2>
    <ol><li>Choose a lesson from the <a href={sitePath('')}>sample library</a> and step through it.</li><li>Use “Edit in playground” to start a separate draft from that sample.</li><li>Edit a step card and choose “Save step,” or switch to YAML. Source typing restarts the preview at Step 0.</li><li>Download your YAML to keep a file you can embed or contribute to the corpus.</li></ol>
    <p>Drafts are saved in this browser when storage is available. Each sample has its own draft; editing it doesn't change the published lesson. Download important work so you have a copy outside browser storage.</p>
    <p>Builder offers line highlights, value badges, assignments, printing, and captions. Choose Number, Text, Boolean, or None explicitly. “New lesson” starts a separate blank draft. Save lesson details or a step to update the draft. New cards enter the YAML and preview only after a successful Save; Cancel drops them. Number display is under Optional settings, and Occurrence appears for repeated expressions or existing overrides. Unsaved entries are kept while switching views, but aren't saved across reloads. Builder and YAML share Undo/Redo. Advanced actions are preserved and remain editable through YAML.</p>
    <p>The source editor highlights YAML and offers completion and hover help for lesson fields. Press Ctrl+Space for suggestions; select a source problem to jump to it. Ctrl+M lets Tab leave the editor. Source checks catch malformed actions; the preview also checks values, scope, and code targets. Downloads preserve your draft text, including comments and incomplete work.</p>
    <h2>Your first lesson</h2>
    <pre><code>{`title: Storing a number
language: python
code: |
  x = 5
steps:
  - line: 1
    badge: { over: "5", value: 5 }
    caption: The expression gives us the integer 5.
  - assign: { var: x, from: badge }
    caption: Store that value in x.`}</code></pre>
    <p>A badge shows a value above matching code text. The next step copies the latest badge into a variable. Quoted values such as <code>"5"</code> are strings; unquoted <code>5</code> is a number.</p>
    <h2>Author the outcomes</h2>
    <p>Code Loupe currently plays authored steps. It doesn't execute the displayed program. When changing input, a condition, or a loop, also update its values, captions, and output to match.</p>
    <p>Actions within a step follow a fixed order: line change, console activity, badge creation, conversion, then assignment. Put printing in a later step if it needs a newly created badge. Moving to a different line clears existing badges.</p>
    <h2>Author an input explanation</h2>
    <p>The Builder can create the <a href={sitePath('samples/python/numeric-input/')}>numeric-input lesson</a> through forms. Show a prompt writes without a newline; Show entered text adds the response you choose. A Show a value badge can read Latest console input as text. Convert a value changes the latest badge to your authored result and can optionally move it to a picked expression.</p>
    <p>Flat lists use typed item rows in Store a value and Show a value. Add/remove rows, including all rows for an empty list. Badge sources include whole lists and indexed elements; Save fills the authored contents. Select a list element highlights or clears a cell, and badges can set List selection alongside their value. Update a list element uses a scalar literal or the latest badge. The list-iteration and list-update samples are editable through forms.</p>
    <p>These controls do not execute code or calculate later results. Review the remaining values and captions when changing code or input. Other multi-action steps, append/removal, scoped mappings, dictionaries, calls, and unsupported fields remain available in YAML.</p>
    <h2>Follow a function call</h2>
    <p>Use <code>var</code> for a binding being created or rebound, and addresses in <code>target</code> or badge <code>from</code> for existing locations: <code>numbers[1]</code>, <code>report["score"]</code>, or <code>ada.name</code>. Addresses have no whitespace and at most one accessor. Quote brackets inside YAML flow mappings, for example <code>{"from: 'numbers[1]'"}</code>. To read a shadowed global, use <code>{"from: { target: 'numbers[1]', scope: global }"}</code>. Old location mappings receive migration errors.</p>
    <p>The <a href={sitePath('samples/python/function-call/')}>function-call lesson</a> introduces a Call stack panel. A <code>call</code> step creates a frame with its own parameters and locals. A <code>return</code> step removes that frame and shows its result over the saved call expression.</p>
    <p>Calls and returns each occupy their own step, with an optional caption. Assignments inside a function update its locals; globals remain in the Variables panel. Step backward across a return to inspect the function's locals again.</p>
    <h2>Go further</h2>
    <p>Use the header's Appearance menu for the site's Light/Dark modes. The separate Lesson theme menu offers Paper, Midnight, and Terminal for previews. Both preserve your current step and draft. For an embed, set <code>theme="paper"</code>, <code>theme="midnight"</code>, or <code>theme="terminal"</code> on your player.</p>
    <p>The repository has a <a href="https://github.com/amortimer20/code-loupe/blob/main/docs/tutorial.md">full lesson-writing tutorial</a>, a <a href="https://github.com/amortimer20/code-loupe#lesson-format">format reference</a>, and a <a href="https://github.com/amortimer20/code-loupe/blob/main/docs/samples.md">guide to contributing samples</a>.</p>
    <a class="button" href={sitePath('playground/')}>Try the playground →</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
